            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "The dict_extract node takes a dictionary apart: one outlet per key you name, each sending the value filed under that key.\n\n\"dict_extract x y\" has an x outlet and a y outlet. When a dictionary arrives, each key that is in it sends its value; a key that is missing sends nothing. The outlets fire from right to left - the last key first - so the leftmost outlet is the last to send.\n\nunpack_dict is another name for the same node.\n\nWITH NO ARGUMENTS:\ndict_extract on its own takes its keys from the first dictionary it receives, and grows an outlet for each, sending that first dictionary's values once. The keys it learned are kept from then on.\n\nSYNTAX:\ndict_extract <key> <key> ...\nunpack_dict <key> <key> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\ndict_extract x y confidence\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ndict in:\nThe dictionary to take apart.\n\nkey count (option):\nHow many outlets are shown, up to 32.\n\nkey 0, key 1, ... (options):\nThe key each outlet carries. Changing one relabels its outlet.\n\ninclude key in output (option):\nWhen ticked, each outlet sends its key and value together as a two-element list, rather than the value alone.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\n<one outlet per key>:\nThe value filed under that key.\n\nRELATED:\ndict_retrieve takes out one value, by a key that can change while the patch runs. construct_dict does the reverse of this node.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "The dict_extract node takes a dictionary apart: one outlet per key you name, each sending the value filed under that key.\n\n\"dict_extract x y\" has an x outlet and a y outlet. When a dictionary arrives, each key that is in it sends its value; a key that is missing sends nothing. The outlets fire from right to left - the last key first - so the leftmost outlet is the last to send.\n\nunpack_dict is another name for the same node.\n\nWITH NO ARGUMENTS:\ndict_extract on its own takes its keys from the first dictionary it receives, and grows an outlet for each, sending that first dictionary's values once. The keys it learned are kept from then on.\n\nSYNTAX:\ndict_extract <key> <key> ...\nunpack_dict <key> <key> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\ndict_extract x y confidence\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ndict in:\nThe dictionary to take apart.\n\nkey count (option):\nHow many outlets are shown, up to 32.\n\nkey 0, key 1, ... (options):\nThe key each outlet carries. Changing one relabels its outlet.\n\ninclude key in output (option):\nWhen ticked, each outlet sends its key and value together as a two-element list, rather than the value alone.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\n<one outlet per key>:\nThe value filed under that key.\n\nRELATED:\ndict_retrieve takes out one value, by a key that can change while the patch runs. construct_dict does the reverse of this node."
        },
